						<div data-panel="{&quot;flow-children&quot;:&quot;row&quot;}" class="community_award_ctn" id="community_awards_comment_562546398187460384"></div>
					</div>
					<div class="commentthread_comment_timestamp">
											</div>
					<div class="commentthread_comment_timestamp" title="November 2, 2026 @ 2:29:29 pm PST" data-timestamp="1793658569">
						Nov 2 @ 2:29pm&nbsp;
					</div>
				</div>

				
				<div class="commentthread_comment_actions" >
														<a class="forum_comment_action forum_comment_action_trigger" data-tooltip-type="selector" data-tooltip-content="#forum_comment_actions_menu_562546398187460384">
					<span><i class="ico16 arrow_down"></i></span>
				</a>
			
			<div class="forum_comment_action_menu" style="display: none;" id="forum_comment_actions_menu_562546398187460384">				<a class="forum_comment_action report tooltip" href="javascript:Forum_ShowReportDialog( '103582791474736036', '4847652375936776257', '562546398187387886', '36212821', '562546398187460384' );" data-tooltip-text="Report this post for inappropriate content.  Reported post are flagged for review by moderators.">
				<span class="non_breaking_space">&nbsp;</span><img src="https://community.fastly.steamstatic.com/public/images/skin_1/notification_icon_flag_dark.png?v=1">Report this post</a>
			</div>
							</div>
			</div>
			<div class="commentthread_comment_text" id="comment_content_562546398187460384">
				Just so you aren't left hanging, I'll try tossing my two cents in here: Any video game that incorporates live AI services is going to disappear someday. Everything about AI is a giant money sinkhole, and the general business model is to nickel and dime users with premium currency purchases.<br>You know what you are comfortable spending, and I don't really care to tell you what you should or shouldn't do with your money. But to answer your question about future proofing, I don't think that's something that any AI-centric developer would want to entertain overly much. The developer is constantly burning money for as long as the game remains up, so from a business perspective, this game needs to make a lot of cash as fast as possible and they can't have people opt out of paying for anything if they can help it.<br><br>The developer is playing chicken with the consumer, watching their bank account rise and fall month by month, trying to determine when the best moment to pull out and secure their winnings will be. If people are using the AI without finding any excuse to throw money at the developer for more premium currency, then it actually ends up losing them money just to leave it up. In the best case scenario, enough whales get hooked that they keep feeding the developer fistfuls of cash month after month, paying enough for all the freemium users to coast by, cushioning whatever financial blow they're producing for the dev.<br><br>AI microtransactions aren't all that different from loot boxes, you roll the dice, maybe you get something that gives you a small dopamine spike if you get a good roll, and then you go back to grinding. Instead of gun skins or digital cards, you get a paragraph of text that miiiiight make sense and miiiight be pleasant to read, and will probably look very similar to the other eight paragraphs of text you've been given up that point since AI has a habit of homogenizing everything.<br>I guess what I'm saying is that what you're paying for here is even more ephemeral than what you get out of a gacha or loot box, so concerns about &quot;future proofing&quot; might be missing the forest through the trees.<br><br>If it's the gameplay you're after, I personally feel like you'd probably get more out of games like Elin or Creator of Another World, because I'm fairly confident in saying that you won't find satisfaction in what the AI chat bot replies to you with and if you strip the game of that gimmick, it looks like a lesser version of already existing games. (And again, that gimmick of live AI interactions is predicated on constant premium currency purchases to keep the developer from pulling out with whatever earnings they've secured before the AI services drain their wallet dry, so it's best to evaluate the game itself rather than the temporary gimmick it's trying to sell folks on.)			</div>
			<div class="comment_footer_ctn">
									<div></div>
				
				
